\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{A threshold law for discount pricing.}{5}{section*.9}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Channel decomposition of the clone win-rate $\sigma $ (gold vs.\ synthetic clone, 300 duels per row; paired flips vs.\ the unmodified clone).\relax }}{5}{table.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{tab:mech}{{1}{5}{Channel decomposition of the clone win-rate $\sigma $ (gold vs.\^^Msynthetic clone, 300 duels per row; paired flips vs.\ the unmodified clone).\relax }{table.caption.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The erosion mechanism is preferential, not perceptual. (a)\nonbreakingspace Channel decomposition of the clone win-rate: price parity and rating parity each drop $\sigma $ from $0.523$ to $0.386$; full parity drops it to $0.250$, below the plain-gloss baseline. (b)\nonbreakingspace Pricing threshold law: the same clone is rejected at price parity but majority-preferred at any discount $\geq 10\%$; deeper discounts saturate near the mixed-discount pool level. Error bars: Wilson 95\% CIs; dotted line: chance.\relax }}{5}{figure.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mech}{{1}{5}{The erosion mechanism is preferential, not perceptual. (a)~Channel decomposition of the clone win-rate: price parity and rating parity each drop $\sigma $ from $0.523$ to $0.386$; full parity drops it to $0.250$, below the plain-gloss baseline. (b)~Pricing threshold law: the same clone is rejected at price parity but majority-preferred at any discount $\geq 10\%$; deeper discounts saturate near the mixed-discount pool level. Error bars: Wilson 95\% CIs; dotted line: chance.\relax }{figure.caption.10}{}}
